Durarara!! AU
Chapter 5 
Curtis walked with no particular destination.
The late afternoon streets of Ikebukuro moved around him while he kept his own pace, long black braids shifting against the ruined white shirt with every step. Fresh stitches pulled occasionally at his shoulder and scalp. Blood had dried in dark patches along the fabric. He ignored all of it the same way he ignored the stares from people who noticed the state of him.
Somewhere between one block and the next he started humming—low, tuneless, just enough sound to fill the quiet left behind after leaving Shinra’s apartment.
Then he stopped.
A motorcycle shop sat on the corner, its wide front windows filled with chrome and heavy American steel. Most of the floor was taken up by domestic models, but the far side of the display held a small row of genuine USA Harley-Davidson imports—low-slung, loud even while silent, the kind of bikes that looked like they had opinions about the road.
Curtis’s mismatched eyes lit up.
The closed-off expression he had worn since walking out of the apartment cracked for the first time in hours. He stepped closer to the glass, almost without deciding to, and let out a quiet, genuine sound of appreciation.
“…Wow.”
He stood there for a long moment, bloodied and stitched and still carrying everything he had refused to explain, staring at the Harleys like they were the first familiar thing he had seen all day.
The seller inside the shop finally noticed him.
The man’s expression went from polite interest to a clear, open frown the moment he took in the full picture—blood-stiff white shirt, visible stitches on the scalp, dried blood along the collar, the general look of someone who had walked straight out of a street fight and into a showroom. He started moving toward the door.
Curtis looked down at himself.
“…Ah shit.”
The realization hit at the same time as the seller’s approaching scowl. He was not getting anywhere near those Harleys looking like a walking crime scene.
Curtis turned on his heel without another word and started running.
Not full sprint—just a steady, purposeful pace that put distance between him and the shop while the long black braids streamed behind him. The stitches pulled. The shoulder ached. He ignored both.
Home first.
Clean clothes.
Then he was coming back for a motorcycle.
The thought settled in with surprising clarity as he cut down a side street, already mapping the fastest route to the flat he still hadn’t properly slept in. For the first time since leaving Shinra’s apartment, he had a concrete next step that didn’t involve answering questions or sitting still.
He wanted one of those bikes.
Curtis didn’t slow down, but he pulled his phone from his pocket mid-stride and thumbed open the chat room he had joined sometime during his first night in the city.
The screen lit up with recent activity.
Setton:
TarouTanaka. Stay away from the Dollars.
They’re not what you think.
Kanra:
Agreed ♪
The Dollars are fun to watch from a distance.
Getting involved is a different story.
Especially for someone as soft and pure as you, TarouTanaka~
TarouTanaka:
I-I was only asking…
I wasn’t going to join or anything…
Setton:
Good.
Keep it that way.
A new message appeared while Curtis was still scrolling.
Hanma:
Hello.
Just that. One word. No emote. No follow-up.
Curtis’s mismatched eyes flicked over the exchange as he cut across an intersection, long black braids bouncing with his pace. The warning about the Dollars lined up a little too well with the yellow-hoodied idiot who had tried to threaten him earlier. Kanra’s playful tone was familiar in a way that made his jaw tighten—he had a strong suspicion who that particular user was.
He didn’t type anything.
He simply watched the chat update for another few seconds, then locked the phone and shoved it back into his pocket, already turning the final corner toward the flat.
Home.
Clean clothes.
Then the motorcycle shop again—this time looking like someone who belonged inside it.
